    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBVBV32lBXNgCkPQ",
      "text": "AudioEval takes a monophonic clarinet recording and its MusicXML score. Acoustic evidence estimates what was played; score alignment determines where it belongs, including partial passages and unintended repeats. Each reported error links a written location to a recording interval for replay."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBVBV32lBXNgCkPQ",
      "width": 1365
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBVBV32lBXNgCkPQ",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 53,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBVBV32lBXNgCkPQ",
      "left": 70,
      "top": 775
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBMnsH1mV7fsK996",
      "text": "Practising alone requires more than detecting a wrong sound: the player needs to find it in the score and hear the relevant passage again.\n\nAudioEval connects transcription, score alignment and error verification. It returns wrong, missed and extra notes, plus unintended repetitions. Repeated visits retain their original score identities and separate replay intervals.\n\nThe current method uses dual-resolution CTC transcription and bounded passage alignment. Unreliable decisions are withheld as uncertain or unassessed.\n\nThe historical evaluation favoured AudioEval over Polytune and LadderSym, but it does not measure this revised pipeline."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBMnsH1mV7fsK996",
      "width": 1400
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBMnsH1mV7fsK996",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 53,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBMnsH1mV7fsK996",
      "left": 1555,
      "top": 775
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBjMD76RKKYRqTZG",
      "text": "SYNTHETIC SUPERVISION\n\nModified scores introduce wrong, missed and extra notes, timing changes and repetitions before clarinet SoundFont rendering. Each performed event retains its relationship to the intended score.\n\nTranscription learns the notes actually played, including mistakes. These known relationships also support alignment and error verification.\n\nThe historical model used 4,544 training recordings. Its 358 validation recordings were separated by source work and score content. The current acoustic model uses additional synthetic families; these historical counts are not its corpus totals."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBjMD76RKKYRqTZG",
      "width": 1370
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBjMD76RKKYRqTZG",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 51,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBjMD76RKKYRqTZG",
      "left": 70,
      "top": 3210
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBZPb58vQN8r1PKh",
      "text": "The paper reports two comparisons of earlier AudioEval configurations. Synthetic validation assesses score correspondence and event labels; real recordings assess the type and timing of errors."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBZPb58vQN8r1PKh",
      "width": 1395
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBZPb58vQN8r1PKh",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 53,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBZPb58vQN8r1PKh",
      "left": 3050,
      "top": 775
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LB82xbqZZhC3sJnS",
      "text": "PRACTICAL STRENGTHS\nScore identities and replay times make feedback actionable. Repeat tracking distinguishes a later attempt from the first. Acoustic checks can withhold unsupported error calls.\n\nCURRENT METHOD LIMITS\nAmbiguous repeated phrases, same-pitch attacks and bounded searches can cause failures. No rhythm, intonation or timbre labels are currently emitted. An empty error list does not verify a clean performance.\n\nSTUDY LIMITS\nThe current pipeline needs a matched evaluation. The synthetic set was used in development; 21 recordings overlapped baseline training and 3 baseline model selection. Training data and label coverage differ across systems. The real set contains only 11 recordings by one player; rhythm labels remain in scoring although baselines do not predict them.\n\nMUSICPAL: PROPOSED DEVICE\nA touchscreen companion would record a passage, send it with the score to AudioEval, highlight returned errors and replay the audio. Manually reviewed sessions could support future training and evaluation."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LB82xbqZZhC3sJnS",
      "width": 1400
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LB82xbqZZhC3sJnS",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 48,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LB82xbqZZhC3sJnS",
      "left": 1555,
      "top": 2140
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBmfMVQ13DSrl2fn",
      "text": "AudioEval links musical errors to the score and recording. Earlier results support this approach, while the revised method requires independent validation across more players, pieces and recording conditions. MusicPal remains a proposed way to deliver the feedback."
    },
    {
      "type": "resize_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBmfMVQ13DSrl2fn",
      "width": 1400
    },
    {
      "type": "format_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBmfMVQ13DSrl2fn",
      "formatting": {
        "font_size": 53,
        "font_weight": "normal",
        "color": "#193344",
        "text_align": "start",
        "line_height": 1.18
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "position_element",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBmfMVQ13DSrl2fn",
      "left": 1555,
      "top": 3870
    },
    {
      "type": "replace_text",
      "element_id": "PB8dtN2l3nBJwnh9-LBHy5MXlHs8CxHBr",
      "text": "CASE STUDY\nIn Howl's Moving Castle, the earlier system linked a return at 9.8 s to bars 1-4 and identified C6 instead of A5 in bar 12. Human annotations corroborated three repetitions and the wrong note discussed."
    },
